WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
 - You know, when you watch a medical drama on television,

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.620
 there is always that one specific scene.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.080
 - Oh yeah, the alarm's blaring,

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.760
 everybody panicking, right?

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.080
 - Exactly, somebody yells, "Clear!"

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.560
 And they just shock the patient.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:13.920
 I mean, we've all seen it 100 times

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.800
 on shows like ER or Grey's Anatomy.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.000
 - Yeah, it looks like pure chaos,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.440
 but also kind of like magic.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.600
 - Right, you just plug a machine in, zap the chest,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.800
 and the patient is miraculously cured.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.160
 But when you step out of Hollywood

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.920
 and actually look at the clinical science

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.400
 of the human heart, that simplicity completely vanishes.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.400
 - It really does.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.160
 The televised version is, you know,

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.280
 a very binary stripped down look at things,

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.720
 live or dead, shock or no shock.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.040
 - Yeah, and the reality is we're dealing with an engine

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.200
 that is infinitely more complex

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.120
 and honestly way more fascinating

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.400
 than anything you see on TV.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.280
 - Absolutely.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.360
 The real heart is this intricate balance

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.560
 of electricity, plumbing, and chemistry.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.240
 And it's all working together in like

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.000
 perfectly timed milliseconds.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.240
 - Which brings us to our mission

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.760
 for this deep dive today.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.400
 We are essentially giving you a masterclass

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.200
 on decoding the ultimate biological engine.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.040
 - Right, we're unpacking the intense clinical realities

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.320
 of cardiac assessment, dysrhythmias,

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.280
 coronary vascular disorders, and heart failure.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:15.120
 - And by the end of this,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:16.920
 it's not only gonna completely change

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.840
 how you watch those medical dramas,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.440
 but it's gonna give you a real functional understanding

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.520
 of the clinical jargon surrounding your own health.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.480
 - But before we can understand how the heart breaks

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.440
 and how clinicians try to fix it,

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:30.920
 we have to establish a baseline.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.000
 - Right, the normal operating parameters.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.880
 - Exactly, 'cause you can't truly grasp the pathology

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.600
 without first wrapping your head

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.400
 around the baseline physiology, it's just too complex.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.240
 - Okay, so let's unpack this, starting with the blueprint.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.200
 The heart essentially has two main systems

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.640
 working in tandem, right?

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.080
 The electrical symphony and the mechanical plumbing.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.640
 - Right, the electricity tells the plumbing when to pump.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.120
 - And the electrical side is built

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.000
 with this wild cascading fail-safe system.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.360
 You've got the primary pacemaker, the SA node,

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.120
 which naturally fires at what,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.920
 60 to 100 beats per minute?

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.360
 - Yep, 60 to 100, that's your normal resting heart rate.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.960
 - But if that fails, there is a backup,

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.640
 the AV node takes over at 40 to 60 beats.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.160
 - And it doesn't stop there.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.240
 Below that, the electrical signal travels

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:19.120
 down the bundle of hiss through the muddle branches

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.720
 and finally hits the Purkinje fibers.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.120
 - The Purkinje fibers, right.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.600
 And that's the absolute last resort.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:26.600
 - Exactly. - Right.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.920
 - They fire at a grueling 20 to 40 beats a minute.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.840
 Just barely enough to keep you alive.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.360
 - You know, to me, it's exactly like a corporate hierarchy.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:36.240
 - Oh, how so?

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.240
 - Well, think about it.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.200
 The SA node is the CEO, right?

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.680
 Working at top speed, getting things done.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.240
 But if the CEO suddenly quits, the AV node VP takes over,

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.760
 just a bit slower and less efficient.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:49.680
 - Oh, I like that.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.360
 - And if they both quit,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.320
 the Purkinje middle management steps in

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.440
 as a desperate last resort,

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.560
 just trying to keep the doors of the company open

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.560
 at 20 to 40 beats a minute.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.200
 - That is a brutally accurate demotion and efficiency.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.240
 But hey, keeps the company alive.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:06.440
 - Exactly.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:07.840
 - And at the microscopic level,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.560
 every single one of those beats

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.960
 from the CEO down to middle management

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:14.160
 is driven by an action potential.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.720
 - An action potential.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.840
 That's the chemical spark, right?

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.000
 - Yeah, it's a rapid, highly choreographed chemical exchange

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.240
 of ions across the cell membrane.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:27.840
 During depolarization, sodium rapidly rushes into the cell

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.520
 while potassium exits.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.000
 - So that massive shift in electrical charges and polarity,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.600
 that's what creates the actual spark.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.680
 - You got it.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.120
 And then comes repolarization,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.520
 which is the return to a resting state.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.480
 The potassium reenters the cell

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:45.680
 and the sodium gets pushed back out.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.480
 - And within that whole cycle,

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.240
 you have these refractory periods, right?

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.080
 Like moments where the heart can't be shocked.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:53.920
 - Right.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.000
 So there's the absolute refractory period.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:56.840
 During that window,

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.080
 the heart cells are completely incapable

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.200
 of depolarizing again.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.720
 No matter how strong the stimulus is, they will not fire.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:04.560
 - Wow, okay.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.520
 - And then there's a relative refractory period

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.440
 where they could technically fire,

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:11.480
 but only if they're hit with a much stronger

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.000
 than normal stimulus.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.680
 - So why does it need that absolute refractory period?

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.320
 - Well, it's non-digotiable for cardiac function.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.160
 It gives the heart muscle a split second

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.200
 to actually rest and fill with blood.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:24.920
 - Oh, because if it just kept firing,

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:26.600
 it wouldn't have anything to pump.

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:27.480
 - Exactly.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.800
 If the muscle could just be stimulated continuously

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.880
 without a break, it would seize up into a permanent cramp.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.280
 It would move any blood at all,

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.000
 which leads us directly into the mechanical side,

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.160
 the plumbing metrics.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.440
 - Right, because once that electrical spark happens,

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:42.840
 the muscle contracts.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:44.760
 And we measure the efficiency of that pump

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.480
 with a few key metrics.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:47.840
 - Like stroke volume.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:48.960
 - Yeah, stroke volume,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.520
 which is the volume of blood pumped out

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.480
 with each individual beat.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:54.200
 And if you multiply that by your heart rate,

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.560
 you get your cardiac output.

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.960
 - Which is the total volume pumped every single minute.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.240
 - Exactly.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.480
 But there's also the pressure it's dealing with, right?

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:03.400
 - Yeah, the hemodynamic forces.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.320
 We have to look at preload,

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.320
 which is the amount of stretch on the heart muscle

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.920
 at the end of its resting phase.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.960
 - So that's like the maximum volume of blood

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:13.640
 the heart is holding right before it squeezes.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.680
 - Think of it like a water balloon right before you throw it.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.320
 And then there's afterload,

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.640
 which is the amount of systemic vascular resistance

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.440
 the heart has to push against

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.760
 to actually get that blood out into the body.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.840
 - Okay, so if the afterload is high,

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.960
 like if someone has severe hypertension

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.200
 clamping down on their arterial system,

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.080
 the left ventricle has to work exponentially harder.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.440
 - It has to push so much harder

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.360
 just to overcome that resistance, yeah.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.640
 - And the ultimate grade for how well this whole system

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.520
 is working is the ejection fraction, right?

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:42.360
 The EF.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.360
 - Right, the EF is the percentage of blood

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.200
 that leaves the ventricle with each contraction.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.840
 A normal ejection fraction is between 55 and 65%.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:52.840
 - Okay, but if we connect this to the bigger picture,

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.800
 you might wonder why isn't a normal EF 100%,

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.880
 why doesn't the heart squeeze out every last drop of blood?

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.960
 - Well, if the ventricle emptied entirely,

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:04.120
 the chamber would literally collapse on itself.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:04.960
 - Oh wow.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:08.520
 - Yeah, it needs that 35 to 45% residual volume left behind

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:10.560
 to maintain its physical shape.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:12.200
 It essentially primes the pump,

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:13.560
 creating the necessary tension

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.360
 for the next continuous beat.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:16.720
 - That makes total sense.

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:19.960
 Now, when this corporate hierarchy of pacemakers misfires

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:21.680
 or when that plumbing backs up,

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.880
 clinicians can't just guess what's wrong.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:25.040
 - No, they can't.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.040
 That's when the detective work begins.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:29.240
 - And that involves blood, sweat, and tracings.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:32.240
 So let's start with the blood, the cardiac biomarkers.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:33.640
 When the heart muscle is injured,

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:34.760
 like during a heart attack,

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:37.840
 the damaged cells actually burst open, right?

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.320
 - Yeah, they burst and release specific intracellular

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.720
 enzymes into the bloodstream.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:42.880
 - Like myoglobin.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.320
 That one elevates incredibly fast, I saw,

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.040
 within what, one to three hours?

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.280
 - Yeah, myoglobin is fast

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:50.880
 because it's a relatively small molecule.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.680
 When a muscle cell membrane breaks down,

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.080
 smaller molecules just leak out into the circulation

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.960
 much quicker than larger, more complex proteins.

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.640
 - But the catch is it's not specific to just the heart.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:04.160
 - Exactly, any muscle damage can raise your myoglobin.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.080
 That's why they also check things like CK and CKMB,

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:08.440
 which peak a bit later.

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:10.200
 But the absolute gold standard,

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.920
 the definitive lab test for a myocardial infarction

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:13.960
 is troponin.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:16.120
 - Specifically troponin T and I, right?

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:16.960
 - Yes.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:18.400
 - And troponin starts elevating in the blood

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:20.600
 about three to four hours after the injury

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:23.600
 and peaks anywhere from four to 24 hours later.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:27.240
 But the craziest detail to me is that it remains elevated

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.080
 in the bloodstream for one to three weeks.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.320
 - Yeah, it lingers for a very long time.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:34.200
 - But here's where it gets really interesting to me.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.560
 If troponin stays elevated for up to three weeks,

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:39.080
 how do doctors actually know if you are having

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:41.320
 a brand new heart attack today?

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:45.200
 - Or if it's just the old troponin lingering in your blood

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.360
 from an attack you had two weeks ago?

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.120
 - That is a highly dangerous clinical puzzle.

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:53.680
 If a doctor just looks at a single static troponin number,

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:55.280
 they actually can't tell the difference.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:57.480
 - Wow, so what do they do?

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.960
 - That's why they rely on serial troponins.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:02.920
 They draw the blood every few hours to establish a trend.

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:06.560
 They need to know, is the number actively rising right now?

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:09.280
 Is it plateauing or is it slowly falling?

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:10.920
 - Okay, that makes sense.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.720
 - And more importantly, they combine that blood work

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:15.800
 with the physical presentation and the ECG,

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:18.240
 which gives them a real-time electrical map.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.160
 You just can't rely on a single data point in cardiology.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.000
 - Right, and before we dive into the ECG,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.600
 there's also the more active detective work

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:28.640
 like cardiac stress testing.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:30.200
 - The classic treadmill test.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:31.760
 - Yeah, putting a patient on a treadmill

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:35.160
 and having them walk, progressively increasing the intensity

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.040
 until they hit a target heart rate,

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:39.760
 all while monitoring for ischemic changes.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:42.400
 But for patients who physically can't walk a treadmill,

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:44.400
 there's pharmacologic stress testing.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:45.400
 - How does that work?

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:48.160
 - The administer vasodilating agents through an IV

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.680
 to medically mimic the hemodynamic stress of exercise,

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:53.440
 without the patient having to move a muscle.

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:54.360
 - That is wild.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:56.800
 - And if those noninvasive tests show a problem,

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.640
 if they provoke ischemia, then you have to go inside.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.680
 - Right, which means cardiac catheterization.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:04.080
 - The cath lab.

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:05.760
 It's an invasive procedure,

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:08.440
 threading a catheter through the femoral artery in the leg

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:10.680
 or the radial artery in the wrist,

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:12.600
 right up into the heart to physically check

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:14.480
 the coronary arteries for blockages.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:17.760
 - Yes, and the post-procedure nursing interventions

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.320
 for a cardiac cat are incredibly intense.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:21.320
 - I was reading about that.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:24.000
 - Patients have to lay completely flat for two to four hours.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.840
 They have to keep the affected leg absolutely straight.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:27.960
 - It's strict.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.640
 The clinical team is constantly observing the insertion site

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.720
 for bleeding or a hematoma.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:35.360
 They are checking peripheral pulses, temperature,

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.720
 and capillary refill in that extremity over and over.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:40.560
 - What's fascinating here is the sheer physics

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:42.040
 behind those strict rules.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:45.000
 You've just punctured a high pressure arterial system.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:45.840
 - Exactly.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:47.960
 - If the patient bends their leg or sits up too soon,

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.800
 they change the mechanical pressure at that puncture site.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:52.240
 They could easily blow the clot,

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:54.760
 leading to a massive life-threatening

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.320
 retroperitoneal hemorrhage.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:57.480
 - It's terrifying.

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:00.560
 The strictness is entirely about managing that arterial pressure

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:02.400
 so they don't bleed out internally.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:04.600
 - Speaking of pressure, in an ICU setting

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:06.200
 that monitoring gets even more direct

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:08.160
 with hemodynamic monitoring.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:10.880
 They use central lines to measure things like CVP

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:12.680
 or central venous pressure.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:15.960
 And the expected value for CVP is between two and six, right?

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:17.840
 - Right, two to six is normal.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:21.720
 And if that CVP number is high, say 12 or 15,

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:23.600
 it's a clear mechanical indicator

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:25.680
 of fluid overload in the system.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:26.760
 - Because the pump is failing.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:27.680
 - Exactly.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:30.120
 The right side of the heart isn't moving the fluid forward

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:31.600
 efficiently into the lungs.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:34.320
 So the pressure's literally backing up into the venous system.

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:36.440
 It's a plumbing failure.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:38.400
 - So we have the labs showing cell death,

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.000
 the physical stress tests provoking ischemia,

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:42.800
 and the direct pressure readings.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:45.440
 But the bridge between the plumbing and the motor itself

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:47.040
 is that ECG tracing.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:49.240
 - The ECG maps the electrical storms.

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:53.200
 It translates that microscopic cellular action potential

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:54.640
 we talked about earlier.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:56.600
 You know, the sodium and potassium exchange.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:57.440
 - Right.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:01.640
 - It translates that into visible measurable waves on paper.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:05.240
 The PQRS, T, and sometimes U-waves.

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:06.560
 - Which brings us to what happens

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.600
 when those waves devolve into lethal dysrhythmias.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:12.720
 Things like ventricular fibrillation or V-fib,

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:15.920
 and pulseless ventricular tachycardia or V-tach.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:18.240
 - This is where we go back to our TV medical drama,

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:21.000
 but with the actual clinical nuance.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.680
 - Yes, because there's a massive life or death distinction

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:26.480
 between defibrillation and cardioversion.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:28.200
 - A huge distinction.

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:31.800
 Defibrillation is an emergency, unsynchronized shock.

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:34.760
 It's used exclusively for pulseless patients.

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:36.960
 Those in V-fib or pulseless V-tach.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:38.520
 - Okay, so what's the goal there?

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:42.040
 - The goal is to flood the myocardium with electricity.

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:45.960
 Simultaneously depolarizing every single cell at once.

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:47.960
 You're hoping that when they all reset,

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:51.760
 the SA node, our CEO, takes back control.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:53.640
 This is your classic, I'm clear,

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.120
 you're clear, shocking now protocol.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:58.480
 But cardioversion is completely different.

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:00.240
 You're still delivering an electric current,

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:03.120
 but it's highly controlled in times specifically

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:04.320
 with the patient's heartbeat.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:05.800
 - Very specifically time, yes.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:08.320
 - You actually have to press a sync button on the machine.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:09.840
 The patient is usually sedated,

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:13.120
 and they've been NPO, basically fasting for four hours prior.

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:15.200
 Plus, you use a much lower amount of energy,

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:17.400
 usually just 50 to 100 joules.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:19.680
 - Because the timing is everything here.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.720
 The machine reads the ECG in real time

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:24.280
 and waits for the exact peak of the R-wave

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:25.560
 to deliver the energy.

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:27.400
 Why that exact spot?

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:29.960
 - Because if you deliver a shock to read the T-wave,

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:31.960
 which is the repolarization pace,

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:34.920
 you can trigger something called the R-on-T phenomenon,

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:38.360
 and that can instantly throw the patient into lethal V-fip.

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:39.200
 - Oh, wow.

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:40.720
 - Yeah, you're trying to cure an arrhythmia,

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:42.400
 but if you don't sync it properly,

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:45.160
 you could cause a deadly one instead.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.520
 - So, what does this all mean?

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:50.880
 The way I see it, defibrillation is like a hard reboot.

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:53.040
 You're pulling the power cord completely out of the wall

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:54.960
 because the computer has completely frozen

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:56.320
 and you have no other choice.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:57.440
 - That's exactly what it is.

00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:00.120
 - But cardioversion is like a rhythmic nudge.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:01.680
 It's like catching a skipping record

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:05.000
 and gently tapping it at the exact right millisecond

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:07.240
 to put the needle back in the proper groove,

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.960
 carefully avoiding the scratch that's causing the skip.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.360
 - That's a perfect way to put it.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:12.800
 You're nudging the electrical system

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:14.360
 back into its normal rhythm

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:15.920
 without shutting the whole thing down.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:18.640
 - And for patients who have chronic electrical issues,

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:21.200
 they don't just stay in the hospital getting nudged.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:24.720
 We rely on implanted devices to manage this automatically.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:28.760
 - Right, pacemakers to provide continuous electrical stimuli.

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:32.960
 These are programmed with very complex NASPA B-PG codes

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:36.440
 that tell the device exactly when and how to fire.

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:40.160
 - Or they use ICDs, implantable cardioverter defibrillators,

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:41.960
 which sit there monitoring,

00:13:41.960 --> 00:13:45.040
 ready to terminate life-threatening episodes from the inside.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.480
 - But having a machine wired into your heart has risks.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:50.920
 The complications for pacemakers are wild.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:52.560
 - Yeah, I was looking at that.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:55.560
 You've got the standard surgical risks, like infection,

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:58.600
 bleeding, or the lead physically dislocating.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:01.680
 There's also loss of capture where the pacemaker fires,

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:03.800
 but the heart muscle just ignored it.

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:06.600
 - But what's really interesting is the environmental risks.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.720
 - Yes, the one that really stood out to me

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:11.960
 was the risk of interference from microwave ovens.

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:13.600
 Patients literally have to be careful

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:15.600
 around certain kitchen appliances.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:16.600
 - I know, it sounds bizarre,

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:19.240
 but older or improperly shielded microwaves

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.160
 emit electromagnetic interference.

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:22.480
 - And the pacemaker can feel that.

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:24.640
 - Yes, if the pacemaker's sensors pick up

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:26.840
 that external electromagnetic field,

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:27.960
 they might misinterpret it

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.560
 as the heart's own electrical activity.

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:33.320
 The device thinks, oh, the heart is beating fine on its own.

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:34.800
 I don't need to fire.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:37.000
 And it inappropriately pauses.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:38.240
 - That is terrifying.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:40.720
 Just heating up leftovers could trick your heart.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:42.880
 But you know, these electrical storms,

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:46.880
 these chaotic rhythms that require shocks and pacemakers,

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:48.680
 they are very often just a symptom

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:50.960
 of a much deeper structural problem.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:52.040
 - Exactly.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:53.240
 The electricity goes haywire

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:55.720
 because the tissue itself is starving.

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:57.200
 It lacks blood supply,

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.560
 which brings us to the plumbing emergencies.

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:03.600
 - Ischemia, angina and the myocardial infarction.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:06.800
 - It almost always originates with coronary atherosclerosis,

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:09.960
 which is the abnormal accumulation of lipid deposits

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:12.240
 and fibrous tissue within the arterial walls.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:13.640
 - Plaque, basically.

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:14.480
 - Right.

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:17.480
 As that plaque builds, the lumen of the artery narrows.

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:18.800
 Blood flows restricted.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:20.920
 And when the heart muscle demands more oxygen

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:23.040
 than those blocked pipes can deliver,

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:24.480
 you get angina pectoris.

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:25.840
 - Chest pain.

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:27.280
 And you really have to differentiate

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:29.600
 between stable and unstable angina.

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:30.800
 - Yes, that's critical.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:32.440
 - Stable angina is predictable.

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:34.480
 It happens during physical exertion.

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:37.040
 But the crucial part is that it stops with rest

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:40.400
 or with nitroglycerin, which dilates the blood vessels.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:42.840
 - But unstable angina is a massive red flag.

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:45.440
 - Right, because the pain doesn't stop with rest.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:47.960
 And it doesn't stop with nitroglycerin.

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:49.640
 The vessel is critically narrowed

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:52.040
 or a plaque has ruptured and you are basically

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:54.400
 on the precipice of a full-blown heart attack.

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:57.320
 - A myocardial infarction, an MI.

00:15:57.320 --> 00:15:59.640
 This isn't just temporary ischemia anymore.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:03.200
 This is a complete blockage causing irreversible damage

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:05.480
 and cell death to the myocardium.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:08.200
 - And that's when you'll see those distinct EKG changes

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:11.720
 like ST elevation paired with the massive elevation

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:15.800
 of troponin we talked about as those starved cells burst.

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:17.560
 - In the treatments have to be immediate.

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:19.880
 Clinicians use thrombolytics like TPA,

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:23.640
 which is a protein that actively dissolves blood clots.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.600
 - But giving a patient a powerful clot-busting drug

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:27.960
 makes their risk for injury

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:30.280
 and bleeding the absolute number one priority.

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:32.760
 - Oh yeah, because TPA is systemic.

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:35.600
 It doesn't just target the clot and the coronary artery.

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:38.760
 It dissolves every protective clot anywhere in your body.

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:39.640
 - Right, anywhere.

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:42.320
 - Anywhere, an IV site, a recent minor scrape

00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:44.000
 or even a microscopic weakness

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.280
 in a cerebral vessel in your brain.

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:49.280
 All of it is at risk for sudden massive hemorrhage.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:51.720
 - So the monitoring has to be obsessive.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:52.560
 - Exactly.

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:53.560
 - And if drugs aren't enough,

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:56.120
 they are heading straight to the cath lab for a stent.

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:57.680
 The user balloon to compress the plaque

00:16:57.680 --> 00:16:59.400
 and leave a little metal mesh in the artery

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:00.760
 to keep it open.

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:03.680
 - Or in the most severe multi-vessel cases,

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:07.480
 they do a cabbage, coronary artery bypass graft.

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:10.200
 That is open heart surgery to literally harvest a vein

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:11.680
 from the leg or chest

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:13.800
 and build an entirely new plumbing route

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:15.520
 around the blockage.

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:17.480
 - But in the immediate emergency phase,

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:19.080
 you know, before they even get to the cath lab,

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:22.680
 there is the famous Mona protocol, M-O-N-A.

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:26.440
 Morphine, oxygen, nitroglycerin and aspirin.

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:27.960
 - Right, Mona.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:29.520
 The morphine relieves pain

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.680
 and reduces the sympathetic nervous systems

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:33.200
 workload on the heart.

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:36.520
 Oxygen increases the supply to the starving tissue.

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:38.640
 - Nitroglycerin massively dilates the vessels

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:41.960
 to improve flow and the aspirin acts as an anti-platelet

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:44.800
 to stop the clot from aggregating and getting any bigger.

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:46.280
 But I know you noticed something interesting

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:47.600
 about that acronym.

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:49.200
 - I did, I have to point this out.

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:51.200
 They use the acronym Mona.

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:54.000
 - But the actual clinical guidance specifically notes,

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.360
 it does not have to be given in that order.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:58.800
 In fact, since ischemia literally means

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:00.600
 the tissue is starving for oxygen.

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:04.520
 Wouldn't oxygen logically be the actual clinical priority?

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:05.720
 - Oh, 100%.

00:18:05.720 --> 00:18:08.320
 - It feels like Mona is just a catchy memory trick

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:12.000
 for students rather than a strict chronological checklist.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:14.240
 You obviously wouldn't withhold oxygen

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:16.080
 just because you haven't pushed morphine yet.

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:17.560
 - That's a vital distinction.

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:20.520
 In clinical practice, you absolutely don't delay oxygen.

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.760
 The mnemonic is purely cognitive scaffolding

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:25.440
 to ensure all four interventions are executed

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.360
 during a chaotic code situation.

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:28.280
 - Right.

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:30.400
 - It's a prime example of why rote memorization

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:32.320
 always has to be subordinated

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:34.480
 to physiological critical thinking.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:36.960
 You have to treat the patient, not the acronym.

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:39.840
 - So, assuming the patient gets the right interventions,

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:42.720
 they get the oxygen, the TPA, maybe a stent,

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:46.040
 and they survive the MI, the plumbing emergency is over,

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:49.240
 but that's rarely the end of the story, is it?

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:50.960
 - Unfortunately, no.

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:52.280
 Surviving the acute event

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:55.040
 is really just entering a new phase of the disease.

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:56.320
 - Because the heart is damaged.

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:57.160
 - Right.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:59.360
 When the heart sustains permanent damage from an MI,

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:02.720
 or even just from years of chronic high afterload

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:04.440
 pushing against hypertension,

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:06.640
 the muscle undergoes remodeling.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:08.640
 It scars, it stretches,

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:12.040
 and that leads to heart failure, or CHF.

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:12.920
 Heart failure.

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:15.440
 That's the inability of the heart to pump sufficient blood

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:17.800
 to meet the metabolic needs of the body,

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:20.000
 because that muscle is permanently weakened.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:22.280
 The ejection fraction just plummets, right?

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:25.560
 - Yeah, he can't hit that normal 55 to 65% anymore.

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:27.720
 The pump is fundamentally broken.

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:28.920
 - And because the pump is weak,

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:30.560
 you lose that forward momentum.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:32.960
 It becomes a chronic syndrome characterized

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:35.040
 by systemic fluid overload

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:37.440
 in an adequate tissue perfusion.

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:39.040
 - The fluid has to go somewhere.

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:40.960
 So the blood backs up into the pulmonary system,

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:42.920
 causing severe shortness of breath,

00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:45.400
 or it backs up into the peripheral venous system,

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:48.960
 causing massive edema or swelling in the legs and abdomen.

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:50.120
 - And reading through the sources,

00:19:50.120 --> 00:19:52.120
 the major crisis with CHF right now

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:53.400
 isn't just treating it once.

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:56.320
 It's the staggering hospital readmission rates.

00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:58.040
 - It's a huge burden on the system.

00:19:58.040 --> 00:20:00.320
 Patients get stabilized in the ICU,

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:02.320
 they get the fluid pulled off them with diuretics,

00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:03.560
 and they go home.

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:06.280
 But managing this disease as an outpatient

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:08.320
 is basically like walking a tightrope.

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:11.320
 It requires meticulous daily management.

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:13.000
 Things like fluid restrictions,

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:15.600
 daily weights to catch water retention early,

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:16.720
 strict sodium limits,

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:19.280
 and just a complex cocktail of medications

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:21.400
 to manipulate preload and afterload.

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:23.440
 - And there is zero margin for error.

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:25.640
 One highly salted meal,

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:27.560
 or one mistose of a diuretic,

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:29.200
 can send a patient rapidly back

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:31.000
 into acute pulmonary edema.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:33.840
 - It's a chronic exhausting battle for both the patient

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:35.680
 and the healthcare system.

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:37.880
 Going from the microscopic sodium ions,

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:39.720
 rushing across a single cell membrane

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:42.840
 all the way up to the systemic cascading failure

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:44.080
 of the entire pump,

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:46.520
 it is just a lot to wrap your head around.

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:47.800
 - It really is an incredible machine,

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:49.720
 even when it's failing.

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:50.920
 - But before we wrap up today,

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:52.880
 I wanna circle back to something we talked about

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:54.000
 right at the very beginning.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:55.560
 - The biological blueprints.

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:56.680
 - Exactly.

00:20:56.680 --> 00:20:58.440
 We talked about the SA node,

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:01.320
 the AV node and the Purkinje fibers.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.000
 The fact that the human heart

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.440
 has three distinct cascading backup pacemakers

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:10.080
 built into its very design just to prevent sudden death.

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:12.040
 It's an incredible piece of engineering.

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:12.880
 - It is.

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:14.640
 - And it makes you wonder,

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:17.560
 what other critical biological systems in our bodies

00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:20.920
 have this kind of extreme evolutionary redundancy

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:21.760
 built into them,

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:24.400
 just quietly waiting in the dark to save our lives

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:26.520
 if the primary system fails?

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:28.120
 - I really raises an important question

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:29.800
 about human resilience.

00:21:29.800 --> 00:21:32.480
 When you look closely at the body's architecture,

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:34.560
 you realize we are designed

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:37.280
 to survive some truly catastrophic failures.

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:38.640
 - We really are.

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:41.040
 - Well, thank you for joining us on this deep dive.

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:43.520
 We hope you walk away looking at your own pulse

00:21:43.520 --> 00:21:47.020
 and those TV medical dramas just a little bit differently.

00:21:47.020 --> 00:21:47.920
 Until next time.

